 "text": " When a network feels slow, beginners often confuse bandwidth and latency. Bandwidth is the width of the highway, how many megabytes per second can flow at once. Latency is the travel time, how many milliseconds a single packet takes to reach the server. A satellite link can have high bandwidth for downloading a movie, but high latency that makes a live voice call lag. And how do we protect those packets in transit? A firewall filters out unauthorized traffic at the border, while HTTPS encrypts the payload inside the packet, so if droppers on public wifi cannot read your password. Remember, the router still have to read the packet header to deliver it, so encryption hides your message content, not metadata like the destination's IP address or timestamp.",
